## Is gout a disability?

Under the **Equality Act 2010**, a condition counts as a disability if it has a **substantial** (more than minor) and **long-term** (12 months or more) effect on your normal daily activities. The law looks at how you would be **without treatment**. Occasional flares may not be covered, but frequent flares, tophi or joint damage that affect everyday life can be.

## At work

During a flare you may not be able to walk or stand. You can self-certify for the first **7 days** off sick, then you need a fit note. Reasonable adjustments could include:

- working from home, or sitting down, during a flare
- flexible hours for appointments and blood tests
- comfortable footwear rules

## Benefits

Most people with gout will not qualify for PIP. It may help if joint damage or frequent flares affect activities such as **moving around**, **dressing** (including footwear) or **preparing food**, on more than half of days.

{% checker tool="pip" /%}

If you are over State Pension age, look at [Attendance Allowance](/benefits/attendance-allowance/) instead of PIP. It is for people who need help with personal care or supervision.

Free, independent advisers, such as **Citizens Advice** or a local welfare rights service, can help you check what you can claim and fill in forms.
